/// \defgroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories Upgrade modules and their factory functions
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Fifteen of BFME 2's upgrade module classes, from
///        AllowBannerSpawnUpgrade to TooltipUpgrade, the instance factory
///        each one registers with the module factory, and the INI data
///        factories of ten upgrade module data classes.
///
/// An upgrade module is a behavior module that does something to its object,
/// its player or its castle when certain upgrades are present. Object INI
/// lists it like any other module (`Behavior = GeometryUpgrade
/// ModuleTag_...`). \ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle describes how
/// Object::updateUpgradeModules offers an object's upgrades to each module
/// that has not fired and how UpgradeMux::attemptUpgrade applies them.
/// This group documents the code that creates the modules and their data,
/// not the full upgrade logic of every class. BFME 2 registers many other
/// upgrade modules (Zero Hour ones such as ArmorUpgrade, CommandSetUpgrade
/// and SubObjectsUpgrade among them); only the classes listed below are
/// covered here.
///
/// \par Two factories per module name
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance factory,
/// a data factory, a module type and an interface mask; the registrations
/// are listed in `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`. Every name in
/// this group is spelled exactly like its class and is registered as a
/// logic (behavior) module. Every mask includes the bit Zero Hour uses for
/// the upgrade interface plus at least one further bit, which this page does
/// not interpret because its BFME 2 meaning is not confirmed.
/// - The **data factory** runs while INI loads:
///   ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it with the INI reader for
///   each module line. It creates the module data with its constructor
///   defaults and, when the reader is not null, parses the module's block
///   into it with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and the class's static
///   \c buildFieldParse.
/// - The **instance factory** runs when an object is built:
///   ModuleFactory::newModule calls it with the new object and the module
///   data parsed earlier, and it creates the module.
///
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories describes the same flow
/// step by step for the special-power modules; it applies here unchanged.
/// Neither factory is called directly: the module factory reaches them only
/// through the registered pointers.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - One module data object exists per module line per object template. It
///   is shared, read-only in practice, by every object built from the
///   template, and the module factory keeps it in its own list.
/// - One module exists per object whose template lists it, owned by that
///   object. Zero Hour: the object's destructor deletes its modules and the
///   module factory's destructor deletes the module data.
///
/// \par Fields every upgrade module accepts
/// Each data class's \c buildFieldParse adds a shared upgrade table, which
/// corresponds to Zero Hour's UpgradeMuxData table, and its own table; all
/// but ObjectCreationUpgradeModuleData add the shared one first. Read from
/// the retail table, the shared fields are \c TriggeredBy and
/// \c ConflictsWith (upgrade name lists), \c RequiresAllTriggers,
/// \c RequiresAllConflictingTriggers and \c Permanent (booleans) and
/// \c CustomAnimAndDuration. Zero Hour's shared table has \c TriggeredBy,
/// \c ConflictsWith, \c RemovesUpgrades, \c FXListUpgrade and
/// \c RequiresAllTriggers, so \c RemovesUpgrades and \c FXListUpgrade are
/// not among the shared fields in BFME 2. Unlike Zero Hour, which keeps
/// \c TriggeredBy and \c ConflictsWith as text, BFME 2 resolves them to
/// upgrades while the INI is read (UpgradeCenter::findUpgrade): an unknown
/// upgrade name is an INI error, so the upgrades must already be defined.
/// The class pages list each class's own fields, also read from the retail
/// tables.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Every instance factory allocates the module with the global
///   \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory) at the class's size, runs the
///   class's constructor with the object and the module data, and returns
///   the module as the Module base the factory stores. An exception frame
///   around the constructor frees the block if the constructor throws, as a
///   C++ new-expression requires. When the allocation returns null the
///   constructor is skipped and null is returned.
/// - Most data factories have the same frame. Three of the ten documented
///   here, LevelUpUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData,
///   AttributeModifierUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData and
///   CastleUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData, are a shorter form
///   with no exception frame: nothing would free the block if the
///   constructor threw. Their constructors only run the shared base
///   constructor, set the class's virtual table and zero the class's own
///   fields. Apart from that, the steps are the same in all ten.
/// - Parsing happens after construction and outside the exception frame,
///   so an INI error thrown while parsing does not free the new data.
/// - Zero Hour generates both functions inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory pool
///   and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the global
///   \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par Origins
/// MaxHealthUpgrade, ExperienceScalarUpgrade, WeaponSetUpgrade and
/// ObjectCreationUpgrade, whose data classes appear here, exist in Zero
/// Hour. None of the fifteen module classes listed below is in Zero Hour.
/// Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has sources for many of them (for example
/// AttributeModifierUpgrade, CastleUpgrade, DelayedUpgrade, GarrisonUpgrade,
/// GeometryUpgrade and LevelUpUpgrade), so those classes already exist in
/// BFME 1.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their tags, the shared
///   upgrade fields above and each class's own fields. The upgrades
///   themselves are separate \c Upgrade definitions
///   (\ref page_core_frameworks_upgrades) that the modules name in
///   \c TriggeredBy, \c ConflictsWith and their own fields.
/// - Hard-coded: the module names and what each class does with its
///   fields. A new kind of upgrade effect needs a new class and a new
///   ModuleFactory::init registration (\ref page_modding_modules).
/// - \c TriggeredBy and \c ConflictsWith are checked while the INI is
///   read (see above). Many class fields are stored as text (upgrade,
///   object, attribute-modifier and mesh names) and resolved later; for
///   those, this page does not establish when, or whether, a misspelled
///   name is reported.
/// - Multiplayer: upgrade modules run in the game logic and their data
///   comes from \c Object blocks, so every machine must load identical
///   values (\ref sub_rts_model_modding, \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Saves: an object saves each module's state as a block keyed by its
///   module tag (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle); renaming a tag in a
///   mod orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// \c DelayTime (DelayedUpgrade) and \c Delay (ObjectCreationUpgrade) are
/// parsed by INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt, which turns milliseconds into
/// whole logic frames once, when the INI is read
/// (\ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories has the details).
/// ObjectCreationUpgrade's \c FadeInTime is read as a plain unsigned
/// integer with no such conversion, so its unit is whatever the consuming
/// code expects; that code is not described here. A change to the logic
/// rate (\ref page_frame_loop) therefore affects the two kinds of field
/// differently.
///
/// \see ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, ModuleFactory::newModule,
///      INI::initFromINIMultiProc, \ref sub_objects_modules,
///      \ref sub_rts_model_flow


/// \class AllowBannerSpawnUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "AllowBannerSpawnUpgrade"; it has no
///        fields of its own.
///
/// Its data class accepts only the shared upgrade fields (its own retail
/// field table is empty). The class's own upgrade and removal steps only
/// call two shared upgrade helpers that other upgrade modules in this group
/// also call; this page does not describe them. Anything specific to banner
/// spawning is therefore not in these two steps, and the code that provides
/// it is not identified here.
///
/// \see AllowBannerSpawnUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *AllowBannerSpawnUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AllowBannerSpawnUpgrade":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    shared with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AllowBannerSpawnUpgrade


/// \class AttributeModifierUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "AttributeModifierUpgrade": while
///        its upgrade is active, its object carries a named attribute
///        modifier.
///
/// When the upgrade fires, the module runs a shared upgrade helper and adds
/// the attribute modifier named by its \c AttributeModifier field to the
/// object through Object::addAttributeModifierToPool. When the upgrade is
/// removed, and only if the module had fired, it removes that modifier with
/// Object::removeAttributeModifierFromPool and marks itself as not fired,
/// so a later upgrade can apply it again.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own field (AttributeModifierUpgradeModuleData): \c AttributeModifier, the
/// name of an attribute modifier definition, stored as text.
///
/// \see AttributeModifierUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      AttributeModifierUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class AttributeModifierUpgradeModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for AttributeModifierUpgrade: the shared upgrade
///        fields plus the attribute modifier name.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// AttributeModifierUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template.

/// \fn Module *AttributeModifierUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AttributeModifierUpgrade":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's AttributeModifierUpgradeModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AttributeModifierUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *AttributeModifierUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "AttributeModifierUpgrade": creates
///        the module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. This is one of the three data
/// factories in this group without an exception frame (see the group
/// page).
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see AttributeModifierUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class BuildableHeroListUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "BuildableHeroListUpgrade": adds the
///        templates named in a list held by the controlling player to its
///        unit revival tracker (the class name suggests hero templates),
///        and removes them again on removal.
///
/// When the upgrade fires, the module walks a list of object names held by
/// its controlling player, looks each one up with ThingFactory::findTemplate
/// and adds it to the player's unit revival tracker with
/// UnitRevivalTracker::addInitialBuildUnit. It then sets a control-bar
/// flag and runs a shared upgrade helper. Removal looks the same names up
/// and removes each template from the tracker with
/// UnitRevivalTracker::removeBuildUnit. Before removing each template from
/// the tracker, it also goes through the objects on the player's teams and,
/// for objects of a certain kind, removes the template from a per-object
/// list (not described here); it then sets the same control-bar flag and
/// runs a shared helper. Unlike several modules here, removal does not
/// first check that the module had fired. Where the player's name list is
/// filled is not established here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The data class accepts only the shared upgrade fields (its own retail
/// field table is empty): the hero list does not come from this module's
/// INI block.
///
/// \see BuildableHeroListUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *BuildableHeroListUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "BuildableHeroListUpgrade":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see BuildableHeroListUpgrade


/// \class CastleUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "CastleUpgrade": hands an upgrade on
///        to the castle that its object belongs to.
///
/// When the upgrade fires, the module looks the upgrade named by its
/// \c Upgrade field up in the upgrade center and finds its object's
/// \c CastleMemberBehavior module by name. If there is one and the castle
/// object it refers to still exists, the upgrade is passed to that castle's
/// CastleBehavior module and to the castle object. A final step with the
/// same upgrade follows in every case: it looks for objects within
/// \c WallUpgradeRadius of the module's object that pass a fixed kind-of
/// filter and belong to the same controlling player, and passes the upgrade
/// to each one. What the routines that receive the upgrade do with it is
/// not described here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (CastleUpgradeModuleData): \c Upgrade, an upgrade name stored
/// as text, and \c WallUpgradeRadius (real), the search radius of the final
/// step above.
///
/// \see CastleUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      CastleUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class CastleUpgradeModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for CastleUpgrade: the shared upgrade fields plus the
///        upgrade to pass on and a wall-upgrade radius.
///
/// Built once per module line by CastleUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData
/// and shared by every object of the template.

/// \fn Module *CastleUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "CastleUpgrade": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's CastleUpgradeModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see CastleUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *CastleUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "CastleUpgrade": creates the module
///        data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// One of the three data factories in this group without an exception
/// frame (see the group page).
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see CastleUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class CommandPointsUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "CommandPointsUpgrade": adds to the
///        controlling player's command points while its upgrade is active.
///
/// When the upgrade fires, the module hands its \c CommandPoints value,
/// its object's id and its \c RequiredObject filter to the controlling
/// player's command-point bookkeeping, which appends them as one bonus
/// record. On removal, and only if the module had fired, it passes its
/// \c CommandPoints value and its object's id back to the bookkeeping and
/// marks itself as not fired. The bookkeeping then erases the first record
/// whose value and object id both match and stops there. How the filter
/// limits the bonus is decided by the bookkeeping code, which is not
/// described here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields: \c CommandPoints (integer) and \c RequiredObject (an object
/// filter). Each contribution is recorded under the pair of
/// \c CommandPoints value and object id, not under the object id alone, and
/// one removal erases only the first record that matches both.
///
/// \see CommandPointsUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *CommandPointsUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "CommandPointsUpgrade": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see CommandPointsUpgrade


/// \class DelayedUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "DelayedUpgrade": passes its delay
///        to those modules of its object that pass a check against its
///        activation upgrades.
///
/// When the upgrade fires, the module takes its own activation upgrades
/// (from \c TriggeredBy) and walks its object's behavior modules. Each
/// module that offers a particular interface, and for which a check
/// against those activation upgrades succeeds, is given the \c DelayTime
/// value. This page does not identify the interface or the two calls on
/// it, so how the receiving modules use the delay is not described here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own field: \c DelayTime, a duration written in milliseconds and stored
/// as logic frames (see the group page).
///
/// \see DelayedUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *DelayedUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "DelayedUpgrade": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see DelayedUpgrade


/// \class DoCommandUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "DoCommandUpgrade", configured by
///        two command button names.
///
/// Its data class adds \c GetUpgradeCommandButtonName and
/// \c RemoveUpgradeCommandButtonName, both stored as text, to the shared
/// upgrade fields. The names suggest the command buttons used when the
/// upgrade is gained and when it is removed; that reading comes from the
/// field names only, as the module's upgrade logic is not described here.
///
/// \see DoCommandUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *DoCommandUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "DoCommandUpgrade": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see DoCommandUpgrade


/// \class GarrisonUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "GarrisonUpgrade": sets its
///        object's garrison-upgrade model condition and notifies each
///        garrisonable contain module; removal clears the condition and
///        makes a second, different call on those modules.
///
/// When the upgrade fires, the module walks its object's behavior modules.
/// For each contain module that reports itself garrisonable it sets the
/// object's garrison-upgrade model condition (so the art can show the
/// change) and calls a contain-interface method with a true flag. If the
/// object has an AI, a related interface is then given the object's id.
/// On removal it clears the model condition, calls a different
/// contain-interface method, with no argument, on each garrisonable contain
/// module, gives the related interface a null id and marks the module as
/// not fired. Removal is not the first call repeated with a false flag.
/// This page does not identify the two contain-interface methods or the
/// related interface, so it does not say what either call does to the
/// contain module.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The data class accepts only the shared upgrade fields. The effect
/// depends on the object also having a garrisonable contain module, and
/// the art can react to the garrison-upgrade model condition.
///
/// \see GarrisonUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *GarrisonUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "GarrisonUpgrade": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see GarrisonUpgrade


/// \class GeometryUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "GeometryUpgrade": passes its wall
///        and ramp mesh names to its object's draw modules and applies its
///        \c HideGeometry and \c ShowGeometry name lists to the object's
///        geometry entries.
///
/// When the upgrade fires, the module first tests its conflicting upgrades
/// against the object's and the controlling player's upgrades and stops if
/// either test succeeds. Otherwise it runs a shared upgrade helper and,
/// when any of the three mesh names is set, passes the names that are set
/// to each draw module of the object's drawable. When any mesh name is set
/// or either list is non-empty, it records the name of the geometry entry
/// that is active before the change, then applies \c HideGeometry and
/// \c ShowGeometry to the object's list of geometry entries and, after a
/// further step on the object that this page does not describe, lets the
/// object's WallUpgradeUpdate module react if it has one. This page does
/// not describe the helper that applies the lists; that one list hides
/// entries and the other shows them is read from the field names.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (GeometryUpgradeModuleData): \c ShowGeometry and
/// \c HideGeometry (name lists; each line appends to the list),
/// \c WallBoundsMesh, \c RampMesh1 and \c RampMesh2 (names stored as
/// text). The module saves its own state through GeometryUpgrade::xfer.
///
/// \see GeometryUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      GeometryUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class GeometryUpgradeModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for GeometryUpgrade: the shared upgrade fields plus
///        the geometry lists and the wall and ramp mesh names.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// GeometryUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.

/// \fn Module *GeometryUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "GeometryUpgrade": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's GeometryUpgradeModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see GeometryUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *GeometryUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "GeometryUpgrade": creates the module
///        data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// The constructor it runs sets up the two name lists and the three name
/// strings as well as the shared upgrade fields.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see GeometryUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class LevelUpUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "LevelUpUpgrade": raises its
///        object's experience level, up to a cap.
///
/// When the upgrade fires, the module works out how many levels the object
/// may gain: the smaller of \c LevelsToGain and the gap between
/// \c LevelCap and the object's current level, read from its experience
/// tracker. When that number is at least one, it is handed to the
/// experience tracker, and a further per-level step runs that many times
/// on another interface of the object, which this page does not identify.
/// An object already at or above \c LevelCap gains nothing.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (LevelUpUpgradeModuleData): \c LevelsToGain and \c LevelCap
/// (integers).
///
/// \see LevelUpUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      LevelUpUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class LevelUpUpgradeModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for LevelUpUpgrade: the shared upgrade fields plus
///        the number of levels to gain and the level cap.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// LevelUpUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.

/// \fn Module *LevelUpUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "LevelUpUpgrade": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's LevelUpUpgradeModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see LevelUpUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *LevelUpUpgradeModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "LevelUpUpgrade": creates the module
///        data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// One of the three data factories in this group without an exception
/// frame (see the group page).
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see LevelUpUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class RemoveUpgradeUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "RemoveUpgradeUpgrade": when it
///        fires, it takes named upgrades, and whole upgrade groups, away
///        again.
///
/// When the upgrade fires, and only if the module has not fired before:
/// - For each name in \c UpgradeToRemove it looks the upgrade up in the
///   upgrade center (UpgradeCenter::findUpgrade); unknown names are
///   skipped. An upgrade of the first upgrade type (in Zero Hour, the
///   player type) is handed to the controlling player together with the
///   \c SuppressEvaEventForRemoval flag. Any other upgrade is handed to the
///   object itself and, with \c RemoveFromAllPlayerObjects set, also to
///   the player.
/// - For each group in \c UpgradeGroupsToRemove it goes through the
///   object's completed upgrades and hands every one whose group matches
///   to the object, except upgrades that also trigger this module: an
///   upgrade named in its own \c TriggeredBy is never taken away through
///   a group.
///
/// What the player and object routines that receive the upgrades do is not
/// described here; that they remove them is read from the field names. The
/// module then marks itself as fired. Its removal step runs a shared
/// upgrade helper and marks it as not fired.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields: \c UpgradeToRemove and \c UpgradeGroupsToRemove (name
/// lists; each line appends), \c SuppressEvaEventForRemoval and
/// \c RemoveFromAllPlayerObjects (booleans).
///
/// \see RemoveUpgradeUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *RemoveUpgradeUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "RemoveUpgradeUpgrade": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see RemoveUpgradeUpgrade


/// \class ReplaceSelfUpgrade
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories
/// \brief Upgrade module registered as "ReplaceSelfUpgrade": destroys its
///        object and builds one or more objects in its place.
///
/// When the upgrade fires and the name list is not empty, the module
/// remembers its object's position, facing and name and then, among other
/// steps, hides its drawable, takes it off the pathfinding map and destroys
/// it with GameLogic::destroyObject. It then builds each listed object for
/// the same player with BuildAssistant::buildObjectNow, in a row across the
/// old object's facing and with the same angle, moves it to its spot in the
/// row and gives it the old object's name (presumably so scripts that refer
/// to the name find the replacement). Before each one is built, objects
/// that a geometry and kind filter selects near the old position are killed
/// with Object::kill. Containment is carried over for objects of certain
/// kinds: such a container that held the old object receives the new one,
/// and objects of those same kinds that the old object contained are added
/// to the new one.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields: \c ReplaceWith and \c AndThenAddA. Both append object names
/// to the same list in the module data, so the two spellings are
/// interchangeable as far as parsing goes. The module saves its own state
/// through ReplaceSelfUpgrade::xfer.
///
/// \note Retail behaviour: the old object is destroyed before the names are
///       looked up, and every name is looked up before anything is built;
///       a name that is not an object template stops the module there. A
///       misspelled name therefore removes the object and builds nothing
///       in its place.
///
/// \see ReplaceSelfUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *ReplaceSelfUpgrade::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "ReplaceSelfUpgrade": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see ReplaceSelfUpgrade


